                 └───────────────┬────────────────┘
                                 │
                          Quality Gate PASS
                                 │
                                 ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────────┐
                 │        JENKINS SERVER          │
                 │                                │
                 │ Docker Build                   │
                 │       ↓                        │
                 │ Tomcat Container :8080         │
                 │       ↓                        │
                 │ Host Application :8081         │
                 └────────────────────────────────┘
3. Component Responsibilities
Component	Location	Responsibility
GitHub	Source repository	Stores application source code, pom.xml, Dockerfile
Jenkins	Jenkins EC2 server	Automates checkout, build, test, SonarQube analysis, Quality Gate handling and Docker deployment.
Maven	Jenkins EC2 server	Builds the Java project and executes tests.
SonarQube	SonarQube EC2 server	Analyzes source code for quality and security-related findings and calculates the Quality Gate.
PostgreSQL	SonarQube EC2 server	Persists SonarQube project, analysis and configuration data.
Docker	Jenkins EC2 server	Builds and runs the application container.
Tomcat	Docker container on Jenkins server	Runs the generated WAR application.
4. Server Requirements
4.1 Jenkins Server
•	Jenkins installed and running on port 8080.
•	Java installed and configured.
•	Maven installed/configured.
•	Docker installed and usable by the Jenkins service.
4.2 SonarQube Server
•	SonarQube installed and running on port 9000.
•	PostgreSQL installed and configured for SonarQube.
•	Sufficient disk space for SonarQube data and logs.
Note: For this small project, SonarQube and PostgreSQL can run on the same EC2 instance. A separate database server is not required.
5. Project Structure
sonarqube-repository/
├── pom.xml
├── Dockerfile
└── src/
    └── main/
        ├── webapp/
            ├──index.jsp   
6. Phase A — Prepare the SonarQube Server
6.1 Expand and Verify Disk
Verify that the EC2 root filesystem has the expected capacity before installing SonarQube.
df -Th /
lsblk
Note: The project environment used a 30 GB EBS disk and verified that the root filesystem was expanded to approximately 28 GB.
6.2 Install Required Packages
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y unzip wget postgresql postgresql-contrib
Install the Java version required by the selected SonarQube release according to the release documentation.
6.3 Configure PostgreSQL
1.	Start and enable PostgreSQL.
2.	Create the SonarQube database.
3.	Create the SonarQube database user.
4.	Grant the required database privileges.
5.	Keep PostgreSQL restricted to the SonarQube host/internal access.
6.4 Download and Install SonarQube
cd /tmp
wget 'https://binaries.sonarsource.com/Distribution/sonarqube/sonarqube-26.9.0.129388.zip'
sudo unzip sonarqube-26.9.0.129388.zip -d /opt/
sudo mv /opt/sonarqube-26.9.0.129388 /opt/sonarqube
6.5 Create SonarQube User and Permissions
sudo useradd --system --home=/opt/sonarqube --shell=/bin/bash sonar
sudo chown -R sonar:sonar /opt/sonarqube
6.6 Configure SonarQube Database Connection
Edit the SonarQube configuration and provide the PostgreSQL connection information.
cd /opt/sonarqube/conf/sonar.properties

sonar.jdbc.username=<SONAR_DB_USER>
sonar.jdbc.password=<SONAR_DB_PASSWORD>
sonar.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/<SONAR_DB_NAME>
sonar.web.host=0.0.0.0
sonar.web.port=9000
Note: Use the actual database name, user and password created during PostgreSQL configuration. Do not commit database credentials to source control.
6.7 Start and Verify SonarQube
sudo -u sonar /opt/sonarqube/bin/linux-x86-64/sonar.sh start
sudo -u sonar /opt/sonarqube/bin/linux-x86-64/sonar.sh status
Open SonarQube in a browser:
http://<SONARQUBE-IP>:9000
7. Phase B — Configure SonarQube
7. Create SonarQube Token
6.	Open SonarQube.
7.	Click Profile / Avatar.
8.	Select My Account.
9.	Open Security.
10.	Open Tokens.
11.	Enter a token name such as jenkins-token.
12.	Generate the token.
13.	Copy the token and store it securely.
Note: The token is a credential. Do not place it directly in the Jenkinsfile or GitHub repository.
8. Phase C — Configure Jenkins
8.1 Install Jenkins
14.	Install Java required by the Jenkins version.
15.	Install Jenkins.
16.	Start and enable the Jenkins service.
17.	Open the Jenkins web interface on port 8080.
18.	Complete the initial setup and install the required plugins.
8.2 Configure Maven and Docker
19.	Configure the Java installation used by Jenkins.
20.	Configure Maven or ensure the Jenkins agent has Maven available.
21.	Install/configure Docker on the Jenkins server.
22.	Allow the Jenkins service to execute Docker commands.
23.	Verify Jenkins can run Maven and Docker commands.
8.3 Add SonarQube Credential
24.	Open Jenkins.
25.	Go to Manage Jenkins → Credentials.
26.	Select System → Global credentials.
27.	Choose Add Credentials.
28.	Select Secret text.
29.	Paste the SonarQube token into Secret.
30.	Set the credential ID, for example sonarqube-token.
31.	Save the credential.
8.4 Configure SonarQube Installation in Jenkins
32.	Go to Manage Jenkins → System.
33.	Locate SonarQube installations.
34.	Add the SonarQube server.
35.	Provide the SonarQube server URL.
36.	Associate the stored SonarQube credential.
37.	Save the configuration.
9. Phase D — Configure the SonarQube Webhook
38.	Open SonarQube.
39.	Go to Administration → Configuration → Webhooks.
40.	Create a new webhook.
41.	Set the name to Jenkins.
42.	Set the URL to the Jenkins SonarQube webhook endpoint.
43.	Save the webhook.
http://<JENKINS-IP>:8080/sonarqube-webhook/
Note: The webhook allows Jenkins waitForQualityGate to receive the completed SonarQube analysis result.
10. Phase E — Prepare the Maven Project
The project uses WAR packaging and Java 17 in the provided Maven configuration.
<packaging>war</packaging>

<properties>
    <maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source>
    <maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target>
</properties>

<build>
    <finalName>ROOT</finalName>
</build>
44.	Confirm the pom.xml contains the required Servlet dependency.
45.	Confirm the project compiles with the selected Java version.
46.	Confirm Maven can create the WAR.
47.	Confirm automated tests are available where applicable.
11. Phase F — Configure Docker/Tomcat
48.	Create the Dockerfile.
49.	Use an appropriate Tomcat runtime image.
50.	Build the application WAR through Maven.
51.	Copy the WAR into the Tomcat deployment directory.
52.	Build the Docker image
12. Phase G — Jenkins Pipeline
The Jenkins pipeline follows this sequence:
Checkout
   ↓
Build & Test
   ↓
SonarQube Analysis
   ↓
Quality Gate
   ↓
Docker Build & Run
   ↓
Application Validation
13. Phase H — Execute the Pipeline

53.	Open the Jenkins pipeline.
54.	Run Build Now.
55.	Monitor each stage.
56.	Confirm Checkout succeeds.
57.	Confirm Maven Build & Test succeeds.
58.	Confirm SonarQube Analysis completes.
59.	Confirm the Quality Gate returns PASS.
60.	Confirm Docker image creation succeeds.
61.	Confirm the Tomcat container starts.
62.	Open the application using the configured host port.
14. Network and Port Configuration
Service	Port	Server	Purpose
Jenkins	8080	Jenkins EC2	Jenkins UI and SonarQube webhook endpoint
SonarQube	9000	SonarQube EC2	SonarQube UI/API
PostgreSQL	5432	SonarQube EC2	SonarQube database; restrict access
Tomcat	8080	Docker container	Internal application port
Application	8081	Jenkins EC2	External host port mapped to Tomcat
15. AWS Security Group Rules
63.	Allow Jenkins port 8080 only from administrator/required sources.
64.	Allow application port 8081 only from required test/user sources.
65.	Allow SonarQube port 9000 from administrators and the Jenkins server as required.
66.	Do not expose PostgreSQL port 5432 to the public internet.
67.	Prefer private networking between Jenkins and SonarQube where the AWS network design permits it.
